From: alesx2io <96351978+alesx2io@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 20:14:53 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] DisplayEngine: apply contrast without a translation term

The contrast step pivots around mid gray, which adds an offset to the color
transform. The MediaTek hardware composer mishandles offsets: any non-zero
translation (about -0.004 for the presets) turns the whole screen white, so
only the modes with neutral contrast (Default, Vivid, Reading) worked.

Apply contrast as a plain scale. For the preset contrasts the dropped offset
is below one 8-bit step, so the modes look the same and stay in the hardware
color transform (no GPU composition).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../server/display/color/DisplayEngineController.java     | 8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/services/core/java/com/android/server/display/color/DisplayEngineController.java b/services/core/java/com/android/server/display/color/DisplayEngineController.java
index eae6745ef..ac7b592b3 100644
--- a/services/core/java/com/android/server/display/color/DisplayEngineController.java
+++ b/services/core/java/com/android/server/display/color/DisplayEngineController.java
@@ -232,13 +232,15 @@ final class DisplayEngineController extends TintController {
         if (contrast == 1.0f) {
             return;
         }
-        final float pivot = mNeedsLinear ? (float) Math.pow(0.5, SRGB_GAMMA) : 0.5f;
-        final float translate = (1.0f - contrast) * pivot;
+        // Scale only. A contrast pivot needs a translation term, and some hardware composers
+        // (MediaTek) do not handle offsets in the color transform: any non-zero offset turns
+        // the whole screen white. For the preset contrasts the offset is below one 8-bit
+        // step, so dropping it has no visible effect.
         final float[] contrastMatrix = {
             contrast, 0, 0, 0,
             0, contrast, 0, 0,
             0, 0, contrast, 0,
-            translate, translate, translate, 1
+            0, 0, 0, 1
         };
         preMultiply(matrix, contrastMatrix);
     }
